id: date/local/0035
area: date
op: Date.parse, a date-time form without an offset
in: str:"2024-06T12:30"
out: value: str:"2024,5,1,12,30,0,0"
class: divergence
divergence.broiler-js: Local time takes the zone's base offset where a date is made from local fields, set by a local setter or read by the local getters, and the offset in force at the instant where getTimezoneOffset, toString and some of Date.parse's forms read it, so in daylight-saving time, and in the years before the zone's standard time, the local fields, the offset and the rendering disagree, where one offset, the one in force at the instant, serves them all; the difference shows in a zone with daylight-saving time or a historical offset, such as America/New_York, and not under UTC. Answered by Broiler.JS docs/roadmap/SharedLibraries.md, SJL-6.5 (the Date area's record; the correspondence's row L2).
